---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../../components/Section.astro";
import Stage from "../../components/Stage.astro";
---
<Base title="Badge: status labels in pure CSS" description="iVOLT badge: small status labels in subtle and solid colour variants, sized and coloured from the design tokens. CSS only, no JavaScript involved.">
  <article class="docs-prose" data-enriched>
    <h1>Badge</h1>
    <p>A compact label for status or counts. Badges are not interactive; if the label matters to assistive technology, keep it as real text, not colour alone.</p>
    <p class="docs-meta"><strong>CSS:</strong> <code>css/components/badge.css</code> (in <code>ivolt.css</code>).</p>
    <Stage primary name="badge/variants" title="Tones, subtle and solid" />

    <Section title="Classes" num={1}>
      <p><code>iv-badge</code> plus <code>--primary --success --warning --danger --info</code> (subtle background, coloured text) and <code>--solid</code> (filled). Without a variant the badge is neutral.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Common mistakes" num={2}>
      <ul><li>Using a badge as a button; use <code>iv-button--sm</code> instead.</li><li>Conveying state by colour only: include a word such as “Paid”.</li></ul>
    </Section>
    <Section title="When to use a badge" num={3}>
      <p>A badge labels something that is already on the screen: the status of an invoice, the plan of an account, the number of unread items next to a link. It is read as part of the sentence or the row it sits in, so keep the word short and specific — “Paid”, “Beta”, “3 new” — and put the full explanation in the surrounding text.</p><p>If the label has to be clicked, it is a button (<code>iv-button iv-button--sm</code>); if it summarises a number that needs context, a <a href="/components/stat">stat</a> says more; if it announces something that just happened, use an <a href="/components/alert">alert</a> or a <a href="/components/toast">toast</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Customising" num={4}>
      <p>A variant only reassigns the tone locals, and <code>--solid</code> only swaps background and foreground, so any tone combines with <code>--solid</code> without a third class. To make a brand-new tone, set the locals on a class of your own:</p><pre tabindex="0"><code>.badge-teal &#123;
  --iv-badge-tone: #0f766e;
  --iv-badge-tone-subtle: #ccfbf1;
  --iv-badge-on-tone: #ffffff;
&#125;</code></pre><p>Locals: <code>--iv-badge-tone</code>, <code>--iv-badge-tone-subtle</code>, <code>--iv-badge-on-tone</code>, <code>--iv-badge-bg</code>, <code>--iv-badge-fg</code>, <code>--iv-badge-border</code>, <code>--iv-badge-radius</code>. Check the contrast of a custom tone yourself; the shipped tones are tested in both themes.</p>
    </Section>


    <Section title="Class index" num={5}>
      <p>Parts and modifiers of this family that the examples above do not show. The full list per stylesheet lives in the <a href="/reference">reference</a>.</p>
      <div class="docs-scroller docs-class-index">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>iv-badge--primary</code></td><td>Primary tone.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>iv-badge--info</code></td><td>Informative tone.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>iv-badge--success</code></td><td>Success tone.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>iv-badge--warning</code></td><td>Warning tone.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>iv-badge--danger</code></td><td>Danger tone.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>iv-badge--solid</code></td><td>Filled variant: the tone becomes the background instead of the tint.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </Section>
  </article>
</Base>
